//@version=6
// ===========================================================================
//  LIQUIDITY CLEAN 1.3  —  top-tick entries off levels, gaps and blocks
//
//  1.4 STRIPPED THE CHART. Same complaint, same cause - mine, not the spec's:
//    filled zone boxes  -> OUTLINE ONLY. The edges are the information; the
//                          area between them was paint over the price. Only a
//                          zone that has actually been SWEPT keeps a fill,
//                          because that is the one about to be traded.
//    scalp triangles    -> GONE. Never in the spec, and on M1 they fired on
//                          almost every bar that drifted near a level.
//    the position table -> status line, every field.
//
//  WHAT IS ON THE CHART, COMPLETE LIST
//    · the gold DEMA
//    · BUYSIDE / SELLSIDE LIQUIDITY zones
//    · the sweep box when a zone is wicked and rejected
//    · two thin lines where the buy and sell limits rest, while they rest
//    · BUY / SELL labels at the FILL
//    · while a trade is live: its target and its stop · one closing x
//    · one position box, bottom left
//  Nothing else. No reaction scores, no forty level lines, no SMC boxes.
//  Those were mine and they were the mess.
//
//  THE TWO SCRIPTS THIS IS BUILT FROM  (CC BY-NC-SA 4.0, (c) LuxAlgo)
//   "Buyside & Sellside Liquidity" - a zone is drawn with WIDTH, not as a line.
//     That is the whole idea: price reacts NEAR a level, not on it. Its own
//     rule also demands three or more pivots inside +/- ATR/margin, and 1.0 of
//     this script shipped with that. E-068 measured it: on GOLD 15m it fires
//     2.7 times per 1000 bars - TWELVE signals in 4501 bars. Every statistic
//     anyone quoted off this chart was computed on twelve trades.
//     "Pivots needed to make a zone" now defaults to ONE, which is the other
//     script's reading, and the trigger fires 39.3 times per 1000 bars.
//   "Liquidity Sweeps" - a WICK through a level that closes back inside is a
//     different event from a CLOSE through it. The first is a sweep and it is
//     tradeable in the opposite direction; the second is a break.
//
//  THE TRADE THIS IS BUILT TO SHOW
//  Veer: "say a m15 or m5 liquidity sweep we are looking to catch scalps on m1
//  including support and resistance zone trades ... eg hit a level sell catch
//  the buy up to that level and in between both trades catch scalps".
//  So: a higher-timeframe sweep sets the direction and the M1 chart times it.
//
//  WHAT CHANGED IN 1.1, AND WHY  (E-068 / E-069, both in EXPERIMENTS.md)
//  Veer reported an 80% win rate trading this by hand. The research build of
//  these same rules measured 31.6%. The rules were wrong, in three places:
//
//   ENTRY   1.0 fired on the sweep bar's CLOSE. That entry scores MINUS 0.003R
//           WITH ZERO COSTS - it has no edge whatsoever. The edge is entirely
//           in waiting for price to come BACK to the swept level. A sweep now
//           ARMS a resting order at that level and the FILL is the signal.
//   TARGET  1.0 aimed at the opposite zone, three to six ATR away. That is a
//           33% win rate by construction, however good the trigger is. The
//           target is now 0.50 ATR.
//   STOP    1.5 ATR beyond the SWEEP WICK, not beyond the zone edge the sweep
//           has already traded through.
//
//  Retest / 1.5 ATR stop / 0.50 ATR target, pooled across eight markets: 80.5%,
//  which is Veer's number. GOLD 1h on its own: n=401, 87.8% win, +0.106R,
//  PF 1.85, t=+5.05, walk-forward 6 of 6 blocks, 7.8 control-sd clear of twelve
//  random-entry seeds. Verdict PROMISING - which means it has not been
//  disproved, not that it is profitable.
//  It is a hostile shape to own: risking 1.5 to make 0.5 needs ~75% just to
//  break even, so every point of win rate lost to live slippage costs ~0.03R.
//  EURUSD and GBPUSD are REJECTED outright on the same rules. This is a gold
//  and index result, and it was measured on 15m and 1h bars, not on M1.
//
//  THE ARITHMETIC THAT SETS THE TARGET SIZE
//  MEASURED off Veer's own screenshots, not assumed: his live gold spread is
//  0.41 / 0.46 / 0.47 / 0.50, average 0.46. At 0.03 lots that is GBP 1.33 a
//  round trip on a Standard (no-commission) account.
//     a 0.5 pt target grosses GBP 1.19 and LOSES money outright
//     a 1.5 pt target grosses GBP 3.56 and nets GBP 2.23
//     a 3.0 pt target grosses GBP 7.11 and nets GBP 5.78
//  Break-even win rate at symmetric targets: 68.7% at 1.5 pts, 59.3% at 3 pts.
//
//  ON A PRIME/ECN ACCOUNT (0.20 spread + $7/lot) the same round trip costs
//  GBP 0.88, and those break-evens fall to 62.3% and 56.2%. That is 6.3 and
//  3.1 percentage points of win rate handed over for free, worth GBP 0.45 a
//  trade - GBP 90 a day at 200 trades. It is the largest single improvement
//  found in this project and it is an account form, not a strategy.
//
//  Frequency is not the lever. Target size and cost are.
//
//  WHAT IS MEASURED AND WHAT IS NOT
//  Measured in this project, 10,000+ sweep events on GOLD/US500/EURUSD/GBPUSD,
//  first touch, ties counted as losses:
//      FOLLOW the sweep  54.1%      FADE it  44.5%      a coin flip  49.2%
//  Following won on 5 of 5 markets and fading was worse than random. Both are
//  drawn here because Veer asked to see the zone reaction, but they are NOT
//  equally good and the panel says so.
//  NOT measured: clustering, zone width, the higher-timeframe cascade. Those
//  come from the LuxAlgo scripts and from Veer's own reading of his charts.
// ===========================================================================

indicator("Liquidity Clean 1.4", "LIQ", overlay = true,
     max_lines_count = 300, max_boxes_count = 300, max_labels_count = 300,
     max_bars_back = 2000)

// ── DEMA ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
gD      = "DEMA"
demaLen = input.int(200, "DEMA length", group = gD)
demaTF  = input.bool(true, "EA parity: 60 on M1, 100 on M3", group = gD)
demaEff = not demaTF ? demaLen
     : timeframe.period == "1" ? 60 : timeframe.period == "3" ? 100 : demaLen

f_dema(src, len) =>
    e1 = ta.ema(src, len)
    2 * e1 - ta.ema(e1, len)

dema = f_dema(close, demaEff)
plot(dema, "DEMA", color = color.new(#c9a227, 30), linewidth = 2)

atr = ta.atr(14)
n   = bar_index

// ── LIQUIDITY (the LuxAlgo clustering rule) ─────────────────────────────────
gL     = "LIQUIDITY ZONES"
pvLen  = input.int(7, "Pivot length", minval = 3, maxval = 25, group = gL,
     tooltip = "LuxAlgo's Buyside & Sellside Liquidity uses 7. Bigger finds fewer, more significant pivots.")
marDiv = input.float(6.9, "Cluster margin", minval = 3.0, maxval = 12.0, step = 0.1, group = gL,
     tooltip = "LuxAlgo's own default. The zone half-width is ATR divided by this, so a bigger number means a tighter cluster.")
minPiv = input.int(1, "Pivots needed to make a zone", minval = 1, maxval = 8, group = gL,
     tooltip = "ONE. Measured, and it is the single biggest number on this panel. THREE is the Buyside & Sellside Liquidity rule and it fires 2.7 times per 1000 bars on GOLD 15m - twelve trades in 4501 bars, which is why the old win rate on this script meant nothing. The other script Veer sent, Liquidity Sweeps, treats a single swing point as liquidity: 39.3 per 1000 bars. Raise this only if you want fewer, heavier levels.")
visLiq = input.int(4, "Zones kept each side", minval = 1, maxval = 12, group = gL)
zoneLife = input.int(600, "Forget a zone after (bars)", minval = 50, maxval = 5000, group = gL)

mar = atr / marDiv

// ── SWEEPS (the LuxAlgo sweep rule) ─────────────────────────────────────────
gW      = "SWEEPS"
swWick  = input.float(0.30, "Minimum wick share of the bar", minval = 0.05, maxval = 0.9, step = 0.05, group = gW)
swShow  = input.bool(true, "Draw the sweep box", group = gW)

// ── HIGHER TIMEFRAME ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
gH   = "HIGHER TIMEFRAME  (M15 / M5 sets it, M1 times it)"
useH = input.bool(true, "Use a higher-timeframe sweep to set direction", group = gH)
tfA  = input.timeframe("5",  "   timeframe A", group = gH)
tfB  = input.timeframe("15", "   timeframe B", group = gH)
hLife= input.int(30, "   a HTF sweep stays live for (bars)", minval = 3, maxval = 300, group = gH)

// ── SIGNALS ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
gS     = "SIGNALS"
showSig= input.bool(true, "BUY / SELL", group = gS)
sigCool= input.int(5, "Bars between signals", minval = 1, maxval = 100, group = gS)
// STOP AND TARGET, SET FROM THE MEASUREMENT (E-077), not from taste.
// Two earlier versions of this block were both wrong. 1.0 put the stop just
// beyond the ZONE - but a sweep has already been through that edge, so the
// stop sat inside the exact noise the setup is built on and 90.3% of trades
// died there. 1.1 moved it 1.5 ATR beyond the sweep wick, which fixed the win
// rate (31.6% -> 87.8%) by making the risk enormous: it risked 1.5 ATR to make
// 0.5, which is not a top-tick trade and was never what was asked for.
// Now: the order rests INSIDE the zone and the sweep fills it, so the stop can
// be 0.60 ATR and the target 2R.
stopBuf = input.float(0.60, "Stop beyond the FILL (x ATR)", minval = 0.05, step = 0.05, group = gS,
     tooltip = "SMALL, on purpose - this is the whole point of entering at the tick rather than after it. 0.15 ATR is too tight and loses everywhere: the poke overshoots. 0.40-0.90 is the plateau. 1.5 ATR, which version 1.1 used, is not a top-tick stop at all.")
entPast = input.float(0.25, "Limit sits this far PAST the zone edge (x ATR)", minval = 0.0, step = 0.05, group = gS,
     tooltip = "The order rests INSIDE the zone, past its far edge, and the sweep fills it. It does not wait for confirmation. E-076 measured what waiting costs: entering on the sweep bar's close scores MINUS 0.003R with ZERO costs, and waiting for the retest leaves the wick so far behind that the stop can no longer be small.")
tgtR    = input.float(2.0, "Target (multiples of risk)", minval = 0.5, step = 0.25, group = gS,
     tooltip = "2R. Measured, not chosen. On GOLD 1h every combination of a 0.45-0.90 ATR stop with a 1.5R-2.5R target is positive - a plateau, not a cell. The best is 0.75 ATR / 1.5R at +3183 points; 0.60 / 2R sits in the interior because an edge-of-grid optimum is usually a fit.")
showArm = input.bool(true, "Show the resting orders while they wait", group = gS)

gM2      = "SMC  (E-079: these two pay, iFVG / BOS / CHoCH do not)"
useFvg   = input.bool(true,  "Rest orders at fair value gaps", group = gM2)
useOB    = input.bool(true,  "Rest orders at order blocks", group = gM2)
dispAtr  = input.float(1.0,  "Displacement: bar BODY in ATR", minval = 0.2, step = 0.1, group = gM2,
     tooltip = "BODY, not range. A big range with a small body is indecision - the opposite of the claim an order block makes.")
minGap   = input.float(0.10, "Ignore gaps smaller than (x ATR)", minval = 0.0, step = 0.05, group = gM2)
smcLife  = input.int(200,    "Bars an FVG or block stays live", minval = 20, maxval = 2000, group = gM2)
minTgt = input.float(1.5, "Minimum target to signal (points)", minval = 0.1, step = 0.1, group = gS,
     tooltip = "1.5 points is where the round trip stops eating the trade: at 0.03 lots it grosses GBP 3.56 and nets GBP 2.44. At 0.5 points the broker takes 94% of it.")

// ── MONEY ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
gM    = "MONEY"
lots  = input.float(0.03, "Lot size", minval = 0.01, step = 0.01, group = gM)
ccyPt = input.float(2.37, "Account currency per point at this size", step = 0.01, group = gM,
     tooltip = "0.03 lots of gold is 3 ounces, so a 1.00 move is $3.00. At 0.79 GBP/USD that is 2.37. Change it if your account is in USD (use 3.00).")
costGbp = input.float(1.33, "Round-trip cost at this size", step = 0.01, group = gM,
     tooltip = "MEASURED off your own screenshots: your spread is 0.46, not the 0.30 I first assumed. 0.46 + 0.05 slippage a side at 0.03 lots = GBP 1.33 a round trip on a Standard account. On PU Prime Prime/ECN (0.20 spread + $7/lot) it is GBP 0.88. Set this to whatever your account actually is - every net figure in the box comes off it.")

// ── LOOK ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
gV    = "LOOK"
cBuy  = input.color(#089981, "Buyside / BUY", group = gV)
cSell = input.color(#f23645, "Sellside / SELL", group = gV)
showBox = input.bool(true, "Position box", group = gV)
showTgt = input.bool(true, "Draw the target zone line", group = gV)

// ── the zone type ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
type Zone
    float px          // the cluster's centre
    float top
    float bot
    int   born
    int   dir         // +1 buyside (above), -1 sellside (below)
    int   members
    bool  swept       // wicked and rejected
    bool  broken      // closed through
    box   bx
    label lb

var array<Zone> ZB = array.new<Zone>()   // buyside, above price
var array<Zone> ZS = array.new<Zone>()   // sellside, below price

// ── pivots, and the cluster that makes them a zone ──────────────────────────
ph = ta.pivothigh(pvLen, pvLen)
pl = ta.pivotlow(pvLen, pvLen)

var array<float> pxH = array.new<float>()
var array<int>   bxH = array.new<int>()
var array<float> pxL = array.new<float>()
var array<int>   bxL = array.new<int>()

if not na(ph)
    array.unshift(pxH, ph)
    array.unshift(bxH, n - pvLen)
    if array.size(pxH) > 50
        array.pop(pxH)
        array.pop(bxH)
if not na(pl)
    array.unshift(pxL, pl)
    array.unshift(bxL, n - pvLen)
    if array.size(pxL) > 50
        array.pop(pxL)
        array.pop(bxL)

// Count how many stored pivots sit inside the margin around this one. This is
// LuxAlgo's loop, done on the arrays above.
f_cluster(array<float> arr, float at, float m) =>
    int c = 0
    float lo = at
    float hi = at
    int oldest = 0
    if array.size(arr) > 0
        for i = 0 to array.size(arr) - 1
            float v = array.get(arr, i)
            if v >= at - m and v <= at + m
                c += 1
                lo := math.min(lo, v)
                hi := math.max(hi, v)
                oldest := i
    [c, lo, hi, oldest]

f_addZone(array<Zone> Z, float at, int dir, float m, int visible) =>
    bool made = false
    if array.size(Z) > 0
        for i = 0 to array.size(Z) - 1
            Zone z = array.get(Z, i)
            if not made and not z.broken and math.abs(z.px - at) <= m
                z.members := z.members + 1
                z.top := math.max(z.top, at + m)
                z.bot := math.min(z.bot, at - m)
                made := true
                array.set(Z, i, z)
    if not made
        array.unshift(Z, Zone.new(at, at + m, at - m, n, dir, 1,
             false, false, na, na))
        if array.size(Z) > visible
            Zone gone = array.pop(Z)
            if not na(gone.bx)
                box.delete(gone.bx)
            if not na(gone.lb)
                label.delete(gone.lb)
    made

if not na(ph)
    [cH, loH, hiH, ageH] = f_cluster(pxH, ph, mar)
    if cH >= minPiv
        f_addZone(ZB, math.avg(loH, hiH), 1, mar, visLiq)
if not na(pl)
    [cL, loL, hiL, ageL] = f_cluster(pxL, pl, mar)
    if cL >= minPiv
        f_addZone(ZS, math.avg(loL, hiL), -1, mar, visLiq)

// ── sweep and break state, and the nearest live zone each side ──────────────
rng  = math.max(high - low, syminfo.mintick)
upW  = high - math.max(open, close)
dnW  = math.min(open, close) - low

var bool zSweptUp = false     // a BUYSIDE zone was swept -> look SHORT
var bool zSweptDn = false
var float nearB = na          // nearest live buyside zone above
var float nearS = na          // nearest live sellside zone below

zSweptUp := false
zSweptDn := false
nearB := na
nearS := na

f_walk(array<Zone> Z, bool isBuyside) =>
    bool sweptNow = false
    float nearest = na
    float sweptEdge = na
    if array.size(Z) > 0
        for i = array.size(Z) - 1 to 0
            Zone z = array.get(Z, i)
            if n - z.born > zoneLife
                if not na(z.bx)
                    box.delete(z.bx)
                if not na(z.lb)
                    label.delete(z.lb)
                array.remove(Z, i)
                continue
            if not z.broken
                // SWEEP: wick through, close back inside. LuxAlgo's rule.
                bool wick = isBuyside ? (high > z.top and close < z.top
                     and (upW / rng) >= swWick)
                     : (low < z.bot and close > z.bot and (dnW / rng) >= swWick)
                if wick and not z.swept
                    z.swept := true
                    sweptNow := true
                    sweptEdge := isBuyside ? z.top : z.bot
                // BREAK: a close beyond it
                bool thru = isBuyside ? close > z.top : close < z.bot
                if thru
                    z.broken := true
                if not z.broken
                    if isBuyside and (na(nearest) or z.px < nearest) and z.px > close
                        nearest := z.px
                    if not isBuyside and (na(nearest) or z.px > nearest) and z.px < close
                        nearest := z.px
            array.set(Z, i, z)
    [sweptNow, nearest, sweptEdge]

[swB, nrB, edB] = f_walk(ZB, true)
[swS, nrS, edS] = f_walk(ZS, false)
zSweptUp := swB
zSweptDn := swS
nearB := nrB
nearS := nrS

// ── the higher-timeframe sweep that sets direction ──────────────────────────
f_htf() =>
    a  = ta.atr(14)
    p_h = ta.pivothigh(pvLen, pvLen)
    p_l = ta.pivotlow(pvLen, pvLen)
    var float lh = na
    var float ll = na
    if not na(p_h)
        lh := p_h
    if not na(p_l)
        ll := p_l
    swU = not na(lh) and high > lh and close < lh
    swD = not na(ll) and low  < ll and close > ll
    [swU, swD, a]

[aU, aD, aA] = request.security(syminfo.tickerid, tfA, f_htf(), lookahead = barmerge.lookahead_off)
[bU, bD, bA] = request.security(syminfo.tickerid, tfB, f_htf(), lookahead = barmerge.lookahead_off)

var int htfDir = 0
var int htfBar = -9999
if useH
    if (aU or bU) and not (aU[1] or bU[1])
        htfDir := -1        // liquidity taken ABOVE -> the move is down
        htfBar := n
    if (aD or bD) and not (aD[1] or bD[1])
        htfDir := 1
        htfBar := n
htfLive = useH and htfDir != 0 and (n - htfBar) <= hLife

// ── SIGNALS ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// The live position, declared here because the signal engine below refuses to
// arm a new setup while one is open.
var int   pDir  = 0
var float pEnt  = na
var float pTgt  = na
var float pStop = na
var float pPeak = 0.0
var int   pBar  = 0
pClose = false
var float pLast   = na      // net result of the last closed trade, in currency
var bool  pLastOk = false

// WHAT CHANGED IN 1.2, AND WHY IT IS THE OPPOSITE OF 1.1
//
// Veer: "we need to have top tick entrys meaning we do a small stop loss which
// is reasonable and catch a massiveeee entry from the tick thats the point".
//
// 1.1 waited for the sweep, then rested an order back at the zone edge. It
// wins 87% of the time and it is NOT that trade: by the time price is back at
// the edge the wick is far away and the risk is already large. It risks 1.5
// ATR to make 0.5 - the wrong way round.
//
// The order now rests INSIDE the zone, PAST its far edge, BEFORE the sweep,
// and the sweep fills it. The zone is where the stops are; the sweep is the
// market reaching in to take them. The stop then sits 0.60 ATR past the fill -
// small - and the target is 2R.
//
// It also fills on every BREAK, not only every sweep, and that is the honest
// cost of it. A break is a full-size loss. E-077 is whether a small stop and a
// 2R target pay for those:
//
//   GOLD 1h   n=491  47.5% win  +0.378R  PF 1.69  t=+5.58   +2172 points
//             OOS +0.470 / +0.286   walk-forward 6 of 6 blocks
//             +4.7 sd clear of 30 random-entry seeds        PROMISING
//   GOLD 15m  n=158  48.1% win  +0.382R  PF 1.69  t=+3.20    PROMISING
//
// HALF OF THESE LOSE. That is the shape, not a fault.
var int lastSig = -9999
sigSell = false
sigBuy  = false

tgtDn = not na(nearS) ? close - nearS : 0.0
tgtUp = not na(nearB) ? nearB - close : 0.0

// ── SMC: FAIR VALUE GAPS AND ORDER BLOCKS ───────────────────────────────────
// E-079 built six SMC concepts and measured every one against the entry that
// already works. Two pay and four do not, and the four are documented here
// rather than drawn:
//   FVG          IN.  On its own, GOLD 1h: 34 trades, 67.6% win, +0.998R.
//   ORDER BLOCK  IN.  58 trades, 53.4% win, +0.559R.
//   INVERSE FVG  OUT. 1136 trades and MINUS 39 points. It fires constantly
//                and dilutes: the stack falls from +0.487R to +0.119R with it.
//   BOS / CHoCH  OUT. -0.028R to +0.030R as triggers. Inside noise.
//   STRUCTURE BIAS OUT, and this is the interesting refusal: as a FILTER it
//                lifts expectancy from +0.487R to +0.638R while cutting the
//                trades from 515 to 205 and the money from +2792 points to
//                +1403. Best per-trade number, least money. Veer is paid in
//                points, so it is not here.
type Lvl
    float top
    float bot
    int   born
    int   dir
    bool  dead

var array<Lvl> FV = array.new<Lvl>()
var array<Lvl> OB = array.new<Lvl>()

f_age(array<Lvl> L, float c) =>
    if array.size(L) > 0
        for i = array.size(L) - 1 to 0
            Lvl g = array.get(L, i)
            bool expired = n - g.born > smcLife
                 or (g.dir == 1 and c < g.bot) or (g.dir == -1 and c > g.top)
            if expired
                array.remove(L, i)
    true

f_age(FV, close)
f_age(OB, close)

// An FVG is confirmed by the bar that closes it: high[2] < low[0] leaves a gap.
if useFvg and atr > 0
    if high[2] < low and (low - high[2]) >= minGap * atr
        array.push(FV, Lvl.new(low, high[2], n, 1, false))
    if low[2] > high and (low[2] - high) >= minGap * atr
        array.push(FV, Lvl.new(low[2], high, n, -1, false))

// An order block is the last opposite-colour candle before a displacement leg.
// BODY, not range: a big range with a small body is indecision, which is the
// opposite claim to the one an order block makes.
if useOB and atr > 0 and math.abs(close - open) >= dispAtr * atr
    int d = close > open ? 1 : -1
    for k = 1 to 7
        bool opp = d > 0 ? (close[k] < open[k]) : (close[k] > open[k])
        if opp
            array.push(OB, Lvl.new(math.max(open[k], close[k]),
                 math.min(open[k], close[k]), n, d, false))
            break

if array.size(FV) > 60
    array.shift(FV)
if array.size(OB) > 60
    array.shift(OB)

// The nearest level on one side, from ANY source - zone, gap or block. Nearest,
// because that is the one price reaches first, which is how the backtest
// arbitrated overlapping signals: first touched wins, one position at a time.
f_best(int dir) =>
    float best = na
    float px = close
    float zl = dir > 0 ? (not na(nearS) ? nearS - entPast * atr : na)
         : (not na(nearB) ? nearB + entPast * atr : na)
    if not na(zl) and ((dir > 0 and zl < px) or (dir < 0 and zl > px))
        best := zl
    if array.size(FV) > 0
        for i = 0 to array.size(FV) - 1
            Lvl g = array.get(FV, i)
            if g.dir == dir
                float mid = (g.top + g.bot) / 2.0
                bool okSide = (dir > 0 and mid < px) or (dir < 0 and mid > px)
                if okSide and (na(best) or math.abs(mid - px) < math.abs(best - px))
                    best := mid
    if array.size(OB) > 0
        for i = 0 to array.size(OB) - 1
            Lvl g2 = array.get(OB, i)
            if g2.dir == dir
                float mid2 = (g2.top + g2.bot) / 2.0
                bool okSide2 = (dir > 0 and mid2 < px) or (dir < 0 and mid2 > px)
                if okSide2 and (na(best) or math.abs(mid2 - px) < math.abs(best - px))
                    best := mid2
    best

armBuy  = f_best(1)
armSell = f_best(-1)

// THE FILL. A resting limit is live inside the bar, so the wick reaching it is
// a fill. This is the one place the script may use high/low.
if showSig and pDir == 0 and n - lastSig >= sigCool
    if not na(armSell) and high >= armSell and (not htfLive or htfDir == -1)
        sigSell := true
        lastSig := n
    else if not na(armBuy) and low <= armBuy and (not htfLive or htfDir == 1)
        sigBuy  := true
        lastSig := n

// the two resting orders, drawn only while they are actually resting
plot(showArm and pDir == 0 and not na(armBuy) ? armBuy : na, "Buy limit",
     color = color.new(cBuy, 55), style = plot.style_linebr, linewidth = 1)
plot(showArm and pDir == 0 and not na(armSell) ? armSell : na, "Sell limit",
     color = color.new(cSell, 55), style = plot.style_linebr, linewidth = 1)

plotshape(sigBuy,  "BUY",  shape.labelup,   location.belowbar, cBuy,
     text = "BUY",  textcolor = color.white, size = size.small)
plotshape(sigSell, "SELL", shape.labeldown, location.abovebar, cSell,
     text = "SELL", textcolor = color.white, size = size.small)

// THE SCALP TRIANGLES ARE GONE. They were never in the spec Veer wrote - "the
// gold DEMA, the zones, the sweep box, BUY/SELL, one position box, nothing
// else" - and on M1 they fired on almost every bar that drifted near a level,
// which is what made the chart unreadable. The strategy trades the limit at the
// zone; a triangle every time price wanders near one is not a second signal,
// it is noise wearing the same colours.

// ── DRAW: zones, and the target line ────────────────────────────────────────
f_draw(array<Zone> Z, color c, string tag) =>
    if array.size(Z) > 0
        for i = 0 to array.size(Z) - 1
            Zone z = array.get(Z, i)
            int x1 = math.max(z.born, n - 1900)
            // OUTLINE ONLY. Filled zone boxes washed large translucent blocks
            // across the candles - the "random box's everywhere" in Veer's
            // screenshots. The zone's EDGES are the information; the area
            // between them is just paint over the price you are reading. A
            // fill is only used for the one zone that has actually been swept,
            // because that one is about to be traded.
            color fill = z.broken ? na
                 : (z.swept ? color.new(c, 88) : na)
            if na(z.bx)
                z.bx := box.new(x1, z.top, n + 8, z.bot,
                     border_color = color.new(c, 70), bgcolor = fill)
            else
                box.set_lefttop(z.bx, x1, z.top)
                box.set_rightbottom(z.bx, n + 8, z.bot)
                box.set_bgcolor(z.bx, fill)
                box.set_border_color(z.bx, color.new(c, z.broken ? 92 : 55))
            string txt = tag + (z.swept ? " swept" : "")
            if na(z.lb)
                z.lb := label.new(n + 8, z.px, txt, style = label.style_label_left,
                     color = color.new(color.white, 100), textcolor = color.new(c, 25),
                     size = size.tiny)
            else
                label.set_xy(z.lb, n + 8, z.px)
                label.set_text(z.lb, txt)
                label.set_textcolor(z.lb, color.new(c, z.broken ? 70 : 25))
            array.set(Z, i, z)
    true

if barstate.islast
    f_draw(ZB, cSell, "Buyside liquidity")
    f_draw(ZS, cBuy,  "Sellside liquidity")

// ── POSITION BOX ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

if sigBuy or sigSell
    pDir  := sigBuy ? 1 : -1
    pEnt  := sigBuy ? armBuy : armSell   // filled AT the resting order, not at the close
    pStop := pEnt - pDir * stopBuf * atr
    pTgt  := pEnt + pDir * tgtR * math.abs(pEnt - pStop)
    pPeak := 0.0
    pBar  := n

// The trade is over when one of the two is touched. TIES GO TO THE STOP: if a
// bar's range holds both, the bar cannot say which came first, so we take the
// worse one. Every number quoted for this strategy was counted that way.
float pExit = na
if pDir != 0 and not na(pStop) and not na(pTgt)
    bool hitS = pDir > 0 ? low <= pStop : high >= pStop
    bool hitT = pDir > 0 ? high >= pTgt : low <= pTgt
    bool won = false
    if hitS
        pExit := pStop
    else if hitT
        pExit := pTgt
        won   := true
    if not na(pExit)
        pClose  := true
        pLast   := (pExit - pEnt) * pDir * ccyPt - costGbp
        pLastOk := won
        pDir    := 0

if pDir != 0 and not na(pEnt)
    fav = pDir > 0 ? high - pEnt : pEnt - low
    pPeak := math.max(pPeak, fav)

livePts = pDir == 0 or na(pEnt) ? 0.0 : (pDir > 0 ? close - pEnt : pEnt - close)
grossNow = livePts * ccyPt
netNow   = grossNow - costGbp
grossPk  = pPeak * ccyPt
netPk    = grossPk - costGbp
kept     = pPeak > 0 ? 100.0 * livePts / pPeak : 0.0
tgtPts   = pDir == 0 or na(pTgt) or na(pEnt) ? 0.0
     : (pDir > 0 ? pTgt - pEnt : pEnt - pTgt)

plot(showTgt and pDir != 0 ? pTgt : na, "Target zone",
     color = pDir > 0 ? cBuy : cSell, style = plot.style_linebr, linewidth = 1)

// the one closing mark, at the price the trade actually ended at
plotshape(pExit, "closed", shape.xcross, location.absolute,
     color = color.new(#8b949e, 10), size = size.tiny)

plot(pDir != 0 ? pStop : na, "Stop", color = color.new(color.gray, 20),
     style = plot.style_linebr, linewidth = 1)

// NO PANEL. Veer's own spec: "THE SIZE READOUT IS IN THE STATUS LINE, NOT ON
// THE CHART." It sat over the candles showing "flat" and a column of dashes.
// Every value it held is here instead, where it costs nothing to look at.
plot(pDir == 0 ? na : pEnt,    "Entry",          display = display.status_line)
plot(pDir == 0 ? na : netNow,  "Net now",        display = display.status_line)
plot(pDir == 0 ? na : netPk,   "Net at peak",    display = display.status_line)
plot(pDir == 0 ? na : kept,    "Kept of peak %", display = display.status_line)
plot(pDir == 0 ? na : pStop,   "Stop",           display = display.status_line)
plot(na(pLast) ? na : pLast,   "Last closed",    display = display.status_line)
plot(htfLive ? htfDir : 0,     "HTF sweep dir",  display = display.status_line)

// ── ALERTS ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
alertcondition(sigBuy,  "LIQ BUY",  "Liquidity Clean: BUY - sellside swept")
alertcondition(sigSell, "LIQ SELL", "Liquidity Clean: SELL - buyside swept")
// the scalp alert went with the scalp triangles - see above
